'''Okami Tama Center''' is a complex in the central business district of [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Standing at 450 metres tall, it is the city's most recognizable landmark. The tower is the definitive symbol of the Metabolism movement in Adonia, a biomimetic architectural movement that fused ideas about architectural megastructures with those of organic biological growth. The complex is also the largest skyscraper development in Okaiken by floor area. 

The complex was developed by the Okami Tama Corporation at a project cost of about 640 billion Sanese En. Negotiations with landowners and residents on the site started decades prior, and the redevelopment was heavily influenced by the need to maximize vertical density while maintaining biological and organic design philosophies. 

== Design and Architecture ==
[[File:Okami_Skyline_Tama.png|thumb|left|Sunset view of Okami Tama Center standing tall over the Hiryugawa river and surrounding cityscape of Okami.]]
The building stacks three inverted pyramids that evoke the structure of a massive tree. Metabolism developed during a period when the nation questioned its cultural identity and sought radical re-building, linking biological growth with cultural concepts of regeneration. To reflect this, the Okami Tama Center originally used interchangeable plug-in pods that could undergo self-renewal, allowing the city to grow organically like the branches of a tree. However, as urban needs stabilized, developers ultimately converted these pods into a more permanent fixture. 

During the late planning stages, the Sanese government originally wanted to attach a radio antenna at the top of the tower but ultimately went against it to preserve the aesthetic purity of the flat, tree-canopy roofline.

Accompanying the main megastructure are a series of low-rise buildings that create a landscape pergola. The massive {{convert|8.1|ha|adj=on}} footprint is surrounded by lush greenery and features extensive green space, including a central square, sweeping office spaces, a two-level elevated working space, and vast retail areas. According to the architects, the design embodies the concept of a modern urban village.

== Usage ==
The project has a total floor area of {{cvt|861,700|m2}}, including immense office spaces and approximately 1,400 residential units. The main inverted pyramids contain office space in the lower and middle tiers, while high-end residential spaces are located on the upper floors to maximize views of the Okami skyline. 

The lower floors and the flared base also include a supermarket and a multi-language child care facility, as well as an international school. It is projected that the complex houses offices for about 20,000 workers and residences for about 3,500 people.

